---
import PageShell from '../../../components/PageShell.astro';
import VideoPlayer from '../../../components/VideoPlayer.astro';
import { docLinks } from '../../../data/doc-links';
---

<PageShell title="Reflection on Course Preparation">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      One of the most significant areas of my development during my first three years at Doane has been
      my approach to online course design. My experience teaching BIO-125 and BIO-126 provided an
      opportunity not only to teach existing courses but also to critically evaluate whether their
      structures supported meaningful learning in an accelerated asynchronous environment.
    </p>
    <p>
      When I first taught BIO-125 in 2024, the course contained multiple assignments within each module.
      Some required students to complete tasks that did not appear to contribute substantially to their
      understanding of the material. For example, students were asked in one activity to list the learning
      outcomes for a module rather than demonstrate their understanding of those outcomes. The course also
      included considerable grading associated with assignments that were susceptible to simple copying
      online content rather than requiring students to apply biological concepts.
    </p>
    <p>
      My first term therefore became an opportunity to identify what I wanted the course to accomplish. I
      wanted students to encounter a course that was organized, purposeful, engaging, and appropriately
      rigorous while recognizing the realities of asynchronous learning. I wanted students to encounter
      biological concepts through multiple modalities, have opportunities to practice and apply those
      concepts, and understand what was most important for them to learn.
    </p>
    <p>
      As I redesigned BIO-125 and BIO-126, I worked with Erin Doyle over several weeks to develop and
      refine the course learning outcomes. These outcomes were developed with Bloom's Taxonomy in mind and
      became the foundation for the subsequent course design. Once development was complete, I revised the
      <a href={docLinks.syllabus} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">syllabus</a> to provide a comprehensive overview of the topics, assignments, laboratories, and learning
      outcomes addressed in each module. The syllabus I had first used in 2024 and early <a href={docLinks.syllabusHpmy2025} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025</a> did not
      have a breakdown of how the learning outcomes were being measured in each module.
    </p>
    <p>
      The resulting course structure is intentionally consistent. Each of the eight modules contains two
      primary assignments, including an adaptive learning assignment, a module quiz, and a laboratory
      activity with a corresponding lab notebook submission. Modules four and eight also contain the
      cumulative midterm and final examinations. This consistency reduces unnecessary cognitive load
      associated with navigating the course and allows students to focus their energy on learning Biology
      rather than determining how each new module works.
    </p>

    <p class="table-caption">Module components in each module:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="portfolio-table">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Adaptive Learning Assignment</th>
            <td
              >Mastery-based, directs students to textbook or video to improve concepts they’re struggling
              with</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Second assignment</th>
            <td
              >Application-based (where applicable), meant to be AI-resistant, rooted in engaged and active
              learning strategies</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Module Quiz</th>
            <td>Low-stake assessment, can be repeated three times</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Lab</th>
            <td>Hands-on or Virtual lab using Carolina Labs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Knowledge Check and Lab Submission</th>
            <td
              >Students submit lab notebook with lab pictures and answer pre and post-lab questions</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Midterm Exam</th>
            <td>Proctored, timed, cumulative (modules 1-4)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Final Exam</th>
            <td>Proctored, timed, cumulative (modules 5-8)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      I also intentionally reduced the number of assignments students complete. Rather than requiring
      students to submit numerous smaller activities, I focused on fewer assignments with clearer
      purposes. For example, students use Knewton Alta for adaptive learning, allowing them to encounter
      additional practice when they demonstrate difficulty with a concept. Their second assignment is
      generally application-based and asks them to use biological concepts in a context beyond simply
      repeating information. For example, in BIO-125 during the study of biological macromolecules,
      <a href={docLinks.bio125FoodIntake} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">students track their food intake</a> using a freely available nutrition application, review the resulting
      analysis, and respond to questions that require them to connect the information to concepts from the
      course. In <a href={docLinks.bio125DrawMeiosis} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">another assignment</a>, students draw and explain the stages of meiosis. The requirement that
      the drawing be created by the student provides an additional opportunity to represent biological
      processes visually while explaining what occurs during each stage. I incorporated a hand-drawn
      assignment because research suggests that drawing can enhance learning by requiring students to
      actively process and visually organize information. Students must not only illustrate each stage of
      meiosis but also explain it.
    </p>
    <p>
      In BIO-126, students investigating biodiversity select an invertebrate phylum and examine its
      anatomy, development, and evolutionary characteristics. They then create a three- to five-minute
      presentation explaining their findings. The <a href={docLinks.bio126VideoPresentation} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">assignment</a> asks students to communicate biological
      information rather than simply submit a written response.
    </p>
    <p>
      Comprehensive <a href={docLinks.rubric} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rubrics</a> accompany these application-based assignments. I encourage students to review
      the <a href={docLinks.rubric} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">rubric</a> before beginning an assignment because I want the rubric to function not only as a grading
      tool but also as a learning guide. In this way, students can identify the essential components of
      an assignment before they begin their work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Looking ahead, as I refresh these two courses, I plan to adopt a more intentional and balanced
      approach to the use of artificial intelligence. Up to this point, my primary goal has been to limit AI
      use; however, I recognize that AI can also be a valuable tool for enhancing student learning.
      Because students are already using AI and its presence in higher education will continue to grow, I
      want to design assignments that help students use these tools responsibly, critically, and in
      alignment with course learning outcomes. For example, I plan to create assignments in which students
      must fact-check AI-generated information against peer-reviewed literature, encouraging them to
      evaluate accuracy, credibility, and scientific evidence rather than accepting outputs at face value.
    </p>
    <p>
      In addition, I plan to use BoodleBox to help structure AI use within clear instructional guardrails.
      By training the AI tool to operate within the parameters of the course outcomes, I can better guide
      students toward productive and ethical use while also gaining a clearer understanding of how AI
      supports learning in my classes. As part of this redesign, I will also create rubrics that assess
      not only course content mastery, but also the quality of students’ AI-related skills, such as
      critical evaluation, verification of information, and appropriate citation of AI-supported work.
      These rubrics will also include measures to help determine whether submitted work is authentic and
      completed by the student. Overall, another important goal in developing these assignments is to
      thoughtfully enhance, promote, or restrict AI use depending on the learning objective, while reducing
      the misuse of AI and supporting academic integrity.
    </p>

    <h2 class="subsection-title">Designing for the Asynchronous Student</h2>

    <p>
      The non-residential, non-degree seeking, and non-traditional student population served through OLA
      has strongly influenced my approach to course preparation. My students are often balancing academic
      work with employment, families, military service, or coursework at another institution. Because many
      Biology courses are delivered in an accelerated eight-week format, clarity and organization are not
      simply conveniences; they are important components of student support.
    </p>
    <p>
      Not only does each module follows a consistent pattern, there are certain items that I felt were
      essential in my asynchronous, online course:
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="portfolio-table">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Course Intro Video</th>
            <td
              >Short, 2-minute video that explains the layout of the course and first steps to begin</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Announcements</th>
            <td
              >Housekeeping items, other important information that students need to be reminded of</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Study Guide Worksheets</th>
            <td>This is my way of walking with them as they learn through the module</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <VideoPlayer
      src="/videos/course-intro.mp4"
      title="Course Introduction Video"
      description="A brief video walkthrough of the course structure, expectations, and first steps for students."
    />

    <p>
      At the beginning of each course, students receive a short course introduction video that walks them
      through the course structure and expectations. In courses requiring laboratory materials, I use this
      introduction to alert students that they need to order their materials promptly. The first two labs
      of a course that require a lab kit are virtual, this is to accommodate any delays in lab kit
      shipping.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also use <a href={docLinks.module2Announcement} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">module announcements</a> as an asynchronous equivalent of the housekeeping conversations
      that occur naturally in a face-to-face classroom. These announcements identify upcoming
      requirements, highlight particularly demanding weeks, remind students about examinations, and provide
      guidance about how to approach the module.
    </p>
    <p>
      My "How to Do Well in This Course" <a href={docLinks.howToDoWellAnnouncement} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">announcement</a> provides students with expectations for time
      management, preparation, and successful engagement with the course. <a href={docLinks.studyGuideWorksheet} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Study guide worksheets</a> provide
      another layer of support. Although students do not submit these worksheets for a grade, they guide
      students through the videos and readings and help them distinguish essential concepts from
      information that may be less central.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this way, I try to create an online environment that is both structured and flexible. Students
      retain the independence and flexibility that asynchronous learning provides, while the course
      itself provides a clear pathway for moving through the material.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageShell>
